/*
Original work, written for tests/fixtures/nonlinear-timeline (temporal-
consistency mechanism material, 2026-08-03). Flashback-framed structure:
an interrogation-room present tense frame with two FLASHBACK insertions,
each returning via a CONTINUOUS-marked scene, per the markers
extractTemporalConstraints() in server/nvm/analyze/temporal-consistency.ts
actually parses (heading-only FLASHBACK / CONTINUOUS). Temporally clean
as written -- 0 expected contradictions.
*/

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

A metal table, two chairs, a recorder blinking red. DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES, fifties, sets a thin case file down without opening it. Across from her, ANNA VOSS, thirties, still in a borrowed police blanket, hair salt-stiff.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
Start with the weather. People always skip the weather and it's never the part that's wrong.

ANNA
It was calm. That's the part nobody believes. Glass calm.

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

Okonkwo-Reyes clicks her pen once, twice.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
Three of you went out. One of you came back. Walk me through the boat.

Anna wraps the blanket tighter, eyes fixed on the table's scarred edge.

ANNA
The Marlene. My father's boat. Twenty-two feet, nothing special.

EXT. MARLENE - OPEN WATER - DUSK - FLASHBACK

The Marlene sits low and easy on a mirror-flat sea. ANNA, dry, sunburned, laughing, hauls a line. Her brother TOMAS, twenties, stands at the wheel. Their cousin DEV, twenties, dangles bare feet over the stern.

DEV
Your dad know you take his boat out this far?

TOMAS
My dad knows everything and forgives nothing. So no.

Anna throws a coil of rope at him. It misses. Everyone laughs.

INT. MARLENE - CABIN - CONTINUOUS

Below deck, cramped and warm. Anna digs a cooler out from under a bench, tosses cans up through the hatch.

ANNA
(calling up)
Last three, no fighting.

Tomas's voice drifts down, easy, unhurried.

TOMAS (V.O.)
Bring the good ones.

Anna smiles, grabs one more can for herself, starts back up the ladder.

EXT. MARLENE - OPEN WATER - CONTINUOUS

Anna emerges into gray light -- the sky has changed, fast and wrong, a wall of cloud where there was none. Dev is already on his feet, pointing.

DEV
That wasn't there ten minutes ago.

TOMAS
(taking the wheel hard)
Vests. Now. Both of you.

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS

Anna's hands are flat on the table now, pressed still on purpose.

ANNA
The radio said clear through morning. The radio was wrong.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
Radios usually are, out there. What happened after the vests.

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

A uniformed officer sets two coffees down without a word, leaves. Okonkwo-Reyes slides one across.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
Take your time. I'm not the one who has to live in it after.

Anna doesn't touch the coffee.

ANNA
I keep starting the next part and stopping.

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

Okonkwo-Reyes leans back, softer now.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
Then start smaller. What's the last thing you're sure of.

ANNA
Dev's hand. Around my wrist. Before the water took the boat sideways.

EXT. MARLENE - OPEN WATER - NIGHT - FLASHBACK

Chaos: rain sheeting sideways, the boat pitched hard on its side. Tomas fights the wheel. Dev has Anna's wrist in a death grip, both of them thrown against the rail.

TOMAS
(screaming over the wind)
Hold the line! Hold the—

A wave takes the boat broadside. The world tips.

INT. MARLENE - CABIN - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS

Water floods the cabin fast, black and freezing. Anna, submerged to the waist, claws for the hatch. Dev's hand is gone from her wrist. She doesn't stop moving.

ANNA
(choking, barely audible)
Dev! Tomas!

Nothing answers but the groan of the hull.

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS

Back in the present, Anna's voice has gone flat, procedural, the only way she can say it at all.

ANNA
I got out through the cabin window. I don't remember deciding to.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
Nobody decides that. Bodies decide it for you.

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

Okonkwo-Reyes finally opens the case file. A single photo inside -- the Marlene's hull, recovered, split down the keel.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
Coast Guard found her upside down eleven miles east. No sign of your brother or your cousin. Yet.

Anna stares at the photo like it might rearrange itself into a different answer.

INT. HARBOR PRECINCT - INTERROGATION ROOM - NIGHT

Anna finally reaches for the coffee, hands shaking too hard to lift it.

ANNA
Yet. You keep saying yet like it's kind.

DETECTIVE OKONKWO-REYES
(quiet)
It's the only honest word I've got left tonight.

The recorder blinks red between them. Neither reaches to turn it off.
